  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe nor'easter has stalled near the coast of New Jersey as\nexpected and will continue to remain nearly stationary until\nMonday morning. The low will then gradually fill in and weaken\nas it slowly moves across southern New England the rest of\nMonday through Tuesday.\n\nFor our area, storm surge values of 1.0 to 1.5 feet remain on \ntrack. While total water level forecasts come up just short of \nminor flood stage, the Coastal Flood Advisory remains for large \nwave action. Large near shore waves will bring ample splash over\nthat will bring debris onto vulnerable coastal roadways through\nat least tonight's high tide cycle. In addition, dune erosion \nis expected and the most vulnerable locations will see the \npotential for some inundation.\n\nHazardous conditions at the beaches and over the waters \ncontinue through at least tonight. Near shore waves of 10 to 13\nfeet continue, with only a gradual decrease in height late today\nthrough Monday. The High Surf Advisory that runs through this\nafternoon remains unchanged to cover the hazardous conditions \nfrom large waves and strong rip currents. It is important to \nnote that the long duration of a long fetch will create \nopportunities for sneaker waves that will be larger embedded \nwaves in a set and have potential to run farther into shore. \nTherefore, extra caution is encouraged for those venturing to \nthe beach to observed the surf and avoid rock outcrops and \njetties. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs the breadth of the nor'easter increases, bands of showers \nand steady rain (some heavy at times) will continue to rotate \nonshore this afternoon and tonight with a rumble of thunder not \ncompletely out of the question. The most beneficial rainfall is \nexpected across southern NH and coastal Maine. Further inland, \nthe dry high pressure system to the north will occasionally \nridge southward which will limit rainfall amounts, especially \nthis afternoon and evening.\n\nIt will remain quite breezy through Monday, but winds will \ngradually be decreasing. Southeastern New Hampshire and coastal\nMaine will continue to see the highest gusts, mainly in the 25 \nto 35 mph range. However, we could see a few gusts to 40 mph \nearly this afternoon over far southeast New Hampshire. The low- \nlevel jet will begin a weakening process by later this \nafternoon, so peak SFC winds should decrease by this evening \ninto Monday.\n\nNot much has changed regarding rain totals through Monday. Areas\nsouth of the mountains and along the coast should see a \nanother 1 to 2 inches of rain with locally higher amounts over\nfar southeast New Hampshire and far southwest Maine. Here, \nlocalized totals up to 4 inches will be possible through Monday.\nCAMs suggest that precipitation will likely come in waves with \na convective component. Therefore, a stray bolt of lightning may\nnot be impossible along the coast and over the waters. Embedded\ndownpours will also be possible with P-wats in the 90th \npercentile or greater for this time of year. While most of the \nprecipitation will start to taper off this evening and tonight,\nthe upper low will move overhead on Tuesday which may lead to \nsome lingering areas of light rain. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter the nor'easter moves offshore, the 500mb pattern looks to\nfavor the development of a ridge over the eastern US. With high\npressure setting up south of New England, moderating \ntemperatures are expected for mid to late week with mainly dry \nconditions. Some very weak instability could allow for a stray \nshower to generate on Wednesday or Thursday, but most should \nremain dry. \n\nRain chances are a little higher on Friday, as a cold front is \npoised to move southward across New England. However rain \nchances are likely to remain in the slight-chance category, as \nthe front is very weak and any instability will have to overcome\na modest subsidence layer. High pressure then looks to dominate\nthe weather over the weekend.",
